    "rationale": "Startpoint _124902_ is confidently attributed to uart_rx.v:170, the bit_sample register (`bit_sample <= rxd_reg;` in p_bit_sample). Tracing bit_sample's combinational fan-out within this module shows it drives exactly one place: the D-input mux of the `recieved_data` register in p_recieved_data (`recieved_data[PAYLOAD_BITS-1] <= bit_sample`, gated by fsm_state==FSM_RECV && next_bit). That fan-out terminates at a register (recieved_data) that is not on this path \u2014 the report shows only 2 sequential cells (start + end), so no intermediate register such as recieved_data is crossed. None of uart_rx's output ports (uart_rx_valid, uart_rx_break, uart_rx_data) are combinationally driven by bit_sample either; uart_rx_valid depends only on fsm_state/next_bit, uart_rx_break and uart_rx_data depend only on the registered recieved_data. So there is no combinational route from bit_sample's Q, through logic visible in this file, out to any port \u2014 yet the path ends at DFFR_X1 in an entirely different module (wptr_full.v, async FIFO write-pointer logic), meaning the 12 unattributed gates that actually carry the delay (comparator-like NOR4/NAND4/OAI22/AOI211 gates followed by an AND/NAND cluster and then a 3-stage XOR chain typical of binary-to-gray pointer encoding plus a full-flag NOR3) must belong to glue/top-level logic and to the async_fifo pointer module, neither of which is part of this RTL scope. Because the dominant combinational chain is not contained in uart_rx.v: RETIME is unavailable (no register sits inside the combinational cone within this module \u2014 the only sequential elements bracketing the path lie outside our visibility or are not actually on this path), PIPELINE would require inserting a register into logic we can't see and can't verify reconvergent balancing for (e.g. the FIFO's full-flag/gray-code compare, which must stay bit-exact with the write pointer), and RESTRUCTURE would mean rewriting combinational logic that isn't shown